🔁 ConcurrentHashMap vs Collections.synchronizedMap

| Feature       | `Collections.synchronizedMap`                 | `ConcurrentHashMap`                                         |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Thread Safety | ✅ Yes (wrapper synchronizes every call)      | ✅ Yes (built for concurrency)                               |
| Performance   | ❌ Slower (locks **entire map**)               | ✅ Faster (fine-grained locking / CAS)(locks only portions)                        |
| Locking       | One lock on the whole map                     | Java 8+: CAS + lock per bin (not whole map)                 |
| Iteration     | Must **manually synchronize**                 | Weakly consistent — **no extra sync** needed                |
| Null Support  | ✅ One null key + null values (like HashMap)  | ❌ No null keys or null values                               |
| Introduced In | Java 1.2 (`Collections`)                      | Java 5 (`java.util.concurrent`)                             |
| Best For      | Simple / low concurrency                      | High concurrency, servers, shared caches                    |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 1. `Collections.synchronizedMap`

```java
Map<String, String> map = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

// Single put/get are synchronized by the wrapper
map.put("a", "1");

// Compound actions / iteration still need external sync
synchronized (map) {
    for (String key : map.keySet()) {
        System.out.println(key + " = " + map.get(key));
    }
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🚀 2. `ConcurrentHashMap`

```java
Map<String, String> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put("a", "1");

// No manual sync needed for iteration
for (String key : map.keySet()) {
    System.out.println(key + " = " + map.get(key));
}

// Useful atomic APIs
map.putIfAbsent("a", "2");
map.computeIfAbsent("b", k -> "value");
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 What is CAS? (Compare-And-Swap)
CAS is a low-level atomic CPU operation used for lock-free updates:

1. Read current value
2. Compare it with the value you **expected**
3. If it still matches → **swap** in the new value (success)
4. If someone else changed it → fail → retry

```text
expected = 10
newValue = 11

CAS(address, expected=10, newValue=11)
  → if memory still has 10, set to 11 (OK)
  → if memory is already 12, do nothing (FAIL, retry)
```

In Java: `AtomicInteger.compareAndSet()`, `Unsafe`/`VarHandle` CAS, used inside `ConcurrentHashMap`.

Why CHM uses it:
- Many updates can succeed **without locking the whole map**
- If CAS fails (another thread won), retry or fall back to locking that bin

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Internal working

| Feature     | `synchronizedMap`               | `ConcurrentHashMap`                                      |
| ----------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| Locking     | One lock for entire map         | Java 5–7: **segments**; Java 8+: **CAS + bin lock**      |
| Scalability | Poor under many threads         | Excellent under many threads                             |
| Reads       | Also synchronized               | Generally non-blocking / highly concurrent               |
| Blocking    | All threads contend on one lock | Contention limited to affected bin                       |

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ Null handling

| Map Type            | Null Key | Null Value |
| ------------------- | -------- | ---------- |
| `HashMap`           | ✅ Yes    | ✅ Yes      |
| `synchronizedMap`   | ✅ Yes    | ✅ Yes      |
| `ConcurrentHashMap` | ❌ No     | ❌ No       |

`concurrentMap.put(null, "x")` / `put("x", null)` → `NullPointerException`

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ When to use what

| Scenario                                  | Use This                      |
| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Simple app, low concurrency               | `Collections.synchronizedMap` |
| High concurrency / web / thread pools     | `ConcurrentHashMap`           |
| Need null keys/values                     | `HashMap` or `synchronizedMap`|
| Need atomic ops (`putIfAbsent`, etc.)     | `ConcurrentHashMap`           |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) `synchronizedMap` makes each method call safe — **not** multi-step logic (`check-then-act`) unless you sync yourself.
2) Don’t say “always segment locking” — that was Java 5–7; **Java 8+** uses CAS + synchronized bins.
3) `ConcurrentHashMap` iterators are **weakly consistent** (may reflect some updates; no `ConcurrentModificationException` like fail-fast HashMap).
4) Nulls are forbidden in `ConcurrentHashMap` (ambiguity with “missing” vs null under concurrency).
5) Prefer `ConcurrentHashMap` for modern concurrent apps.
